<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<!--
	  Privacy manifest.

	  Required since spring 2024. Without the NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypes entry
	  below, App Store Connect rejects the upload automatically with
	  ITMS-91053 ("Missing API declaration") minutes after it lands, before any
	  human reviews the build.

	  Why UserDefaults is on the list: lib/supabase.ts persists the Supabase
	  auth session through @capacitor/preferences, which is UserDefaults on
	  iOS. Reason CA92.1 is the correct one — "access info from the app itself,
	  or from the app's container, and the access is not for tracking".

	  NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypes is deliberately absent. The App Privacy
	  questionnaire in App Store Connect is the authoritative declaration for a
	  first-party app, and a manifest that contradicts those answers is worse
	  than one that leaves them to it. Wecycle does collect an email address,
	  a name, photos, other user content and product-interaction analytics
	  (GA4) — answer the questionnaire accordingly.

	  NSPrivacyTracking is false: nothing here follows a person across other
	  companies' apps or sites, and there is no ad network or data broker in
	  the build, so App Tracking Transparency does not apply.
	-->
	<key>NSPrivacyTracking</key>
	<false/>
	<key>NSPrivacyTrackingDomains</key>
	<array/>
	<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypes</key>
	<array>
		<dict>
			<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPIType</key>
			<string>NSPrivacyAccessedAPICategoryUserDefaults</string>
			<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypeReasons</key>
			<array>
				<string>CA92.1</string>
			</array>
		</dict>
	</array>
</dict>
</plist>
